  "improvementStatus": {
    "batchOnly": "Batch and read-ahead changes preserve the PouchDB 9.0.0 identity. Recreate replication with the same databases and selectors; stop/restart is separate from checkpoint reset. Large missing-body batches still need payload and memory bounds.",
    "localThenBoth": "Use push checkpoint=source and pull checkpoint=target during the audit so only the local database is checkpointed. On successful completion, publish each verified local last_seq and its existing session_id to the normal pair using Checkpointer. Restoring ordinary both-sided checkpoints and b25 then performed no all-ID push rescan, and new edits propagated both ways.",
    "remainingDesignWork": "Interrupted audit, cancellation, publication failure or partial publication, permissions, concurrent local/remote writes, conflict/tombstone completeness, realistic large documents, mobile resources, and provider payload limits need explicit recovery design and tests before production implementation. No new persistent handoff state has been introduced.",
    "unsafeBridge": "Passing the Fast Fetch cursor as pull since left one of two conflict leaves missing. Ordinary reconciliation from its own checkpoint recovered the missing leaf. Stream durability alone is not full revision completeness."
  },
  "historicalDecision": "The maintainer recalls choosing a large value to reduce the very expensive initial download before Fast Fetch. That is a rational historical explanation, not independently established Git history. Fast Fetch improves body download but does not eliminate the measured first ordinary revision audit. Retiring universal value 60 therefore needs a bounded catch-up policy, while binary-heavy immutable Vaults retain document-count advantages from larger chunks.",
  "verification": {
